there's a lot of structured data out  there perhaps more than any other type  in it's ripe for use with machine  learning let's explore a new tool that  aims to automatically build and deploy  machine learning models on structured  data will it live up to the promise stay  tuned to find out  welcome to AI adventures where we  explore the art science and tools of  machine learning my name is Yu Feng Guo  and on this episode we're going to try  out Auto ml tables one of the newest  Auto ml products that Google has added  Auto ml tables enables you to  automatically build and deploy  state-of-the-art machine learning models  on unstructured data faster than you  could by hand it automates modeling on a  wide range of data types from numbers  and classes to strings  timestamps lists and nested fields  what's interesting about Auto ml tables  is that you can do all of this without  any coding work you just take an  exported CSV file click a few buttons  wait a while and you get a very highly  tuned model on the other end the team  has worked hard to enable the tool to  work for a wide variety of different  data types and it really explorers that  vast space of possible models and hyper  parameters to try to optimize things on  your behalf let's see what using Auto ml  tables looks like the first step is to  import your training data at the import  tab give it a name select the source  either a table from bigquery or a file  on Google Cloud Storage we're going to  use an open data set that describes  marketing outcomes of a Portuguese bank  and we'll try to model whether a bank  deposit will happen this first step  takes a bit of time as the system  analyzes the columns of your data set  once it's complete you'll get to edit  the auto-generated schema and select the  column for predicting on here you can  also update the column type as well as  whether it is nullable or not  the third tab is called analyze this is  a pretty interesting view and I think it  will be useful for those with datasets  that have a lot of columns so you get a  nice overview of their data you can even  click on the different column names to  see some statistics about your columns  after analyzing the data we're ready to  do the training this is where auto ml  really shines because well all you have  to do is click train there are some  options that you can set including a  maximum budget of training hours this  enables you to experiment with your data  but if you want and limit that training  time before committing to a full longer  training run you'll notice that the  training time shown are somewhat on a  long side of things and that's because  it's not only doing model tuning but  also selecting what model to use in the  first place  so as a result there's a lot of things  happening during training but luckily  for us we don't have to do anything time  to just go get lunch or something I  guess and we're back now we're on to the  fifth tab of auto ml tables which is  evaluation if you've used some of the  other auto oil products before this will  look pretty familiar you can see how the  training did as well as metrics about  the models performance and finally in  tab number six we can deploy the model  to get predictions there's even an  editor in the browser that will make  requests to your endpoint so you don't  need to set up a local environment to  make these calls to try and test out  your endpoint notice also there's an  option at the top of the page to toggle  between online predictions via the REST  API and batch predictions which allow  you to upload a CSV file or point to a  bigquery table and get prediction  results on that entire file or table  considering the amount of time it takes  to use auto ml Tables enables you to  achieve a much higher level of model  performance than you could reach  manually so if you have some structured  data you're thinking of doing some  machine learning on use this as an  excuse to try out Auto ml tables to see